go.py recorded a function VALUE in three places: a call argument
(register(h), function_reference_arg), a keyed composite-literal field
(Command{Run: run}, struct_field_reference) and a package-level alias
(var run = helper, function_reference). Two other value positions had no
edge, so a function used only there had no caller:

- an unkeyed composite-literal element: []func(){a, b}, the dispatch
  table, at package level or in a function;
- the right side of a local binding: f := handler, h = pkg.Fn, a
  function-local var g = run.

Both now go through the resolution the keyed field already used, factored
into one closure: a bare name in the caller's own package (WI-bivin), a
name bound locally at the site shadowing it, a selector by its operand
(WI-kibah); a call, literal or closure on the right names nothing. Edges
are `calls` with evidence function_reference, the Go convention for a
function value. The keyed arm's own literal_element child is not counted
twice (parent must be the literal_value).

